          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Inspection of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetism
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxwell%27s+equations"}> Maxwell's equations
            ) readily reveals that — either in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dielectric+medium"} dielectric medium
            or in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"} background field
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            — they may equivalently be (re)written (in fact this is close to their original historical form) as
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                a system of purely “topological”, “cohomological” or “exterior”
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+differential+equations"} partial differential equations
              %p
                on
                %em twice
                the number of physical fields,
            %li
              %p
                a linear “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constitutive+equation"}> constitutive equation
                ” imposing a “duality” relation on the fields
              %p reducing them to the physical degrees of freedom,
          %p
            where it is
            %em only the second but not the first
            part that involves the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"}> background
            ” structure, namely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dielectric+tensor"} dielectric tensor
            characterizing the ambient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dielectric+medium"} dielectric medium
            and/or the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+metric"} pseudo-Riemannian metric
            characterizing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
          %p
            This fact itself is fairly evident (certainly in modern formulations of Maxwell’s equations, see
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Details"} below
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Kottler22a"} Kottler (1922a)
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Kottler22b"} (1922b)
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Cartan24"} Cartan (1924) §80
            %a{:href => "#Dantzig34"} Dantzig (1934)
            and more recently
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#HehlObukhov03"} Hehl &amp; Obukhov (2003)
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Delphenich05a"} Delphenich (2005a)
            %a{:href => "#Delphenich05a"} (2005b)
            (see the comprehensive account by
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#DelphenichBook"} Delphenich (202x)
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Delphenich15"} Delphenich (2015)
          %p
            We point out
            %a{:href => "#RRFieldInGravitationalBackground"} below
            (see also
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Freed02"} Freed (2002)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            replaced by the (hypothetical) “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"}> RR-field
            ” and then often called the “democratic” instead of “premetric” formulation – is secretly at the heart of the the widely recognized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjecture"} conjecture
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+classification+of+D-brane+charge"} K-theory classification of D-brane charge
            as well as of related conjectures, such as
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Hypothesis+H"} Hypothesis H
          %h2#Details Premetric electromagnetism
          %h3#electromagnetism_in_gravitational_backgrounds Electromagnetism in gravitational backgrounds
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetism
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxwell%27s+equations"}> Maxwell's equations
            ) on any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime+manifold"} spacetime manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,g)
            read, in modern
            = succeed "-formulation:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
          #eq:MaxwellEquationsInTraditionalDifferentialForm.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtable{:columnalign => "right center left", :displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                    %mtr
                      %mtd
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi F
                      %mtd
                        %mo =
                      %mtd
                        %mn 0
                        %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                          %mrow
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mo ,
                    %mtr
                      %mtd
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %msub
                          %mo ⋆
                          %mi g
                        %mi F
                      %mtd
                        %mo =
                      %mtd
                        %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{array}{rcl}
                  \\mathrm{d}\,  F &amp;=&amp; 0 \mathrlap{\,,}
                  \\\
                  \\mathrm{d} \, \star_g F &amp;=&amp; j
                  \\end{array}
          %p where
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+2-form"} differential 2-form
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ∈
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \,\in\, \Omega^2(X)
                is the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Faraday+tensor"} Faraday tensor
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+3-form"} differential 3-form
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ∈
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j \,\in\, \Omega^3(X)
                is the electromagnetic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/current"} current
                density
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⟶
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mrow
                          %mi p
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d} \,\colon\,\Omega^p(X) \longrightarrow \Omega^{p+1}(X)
                denotes the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+differential"} de Rham differential
          %p and last not least
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ⋆
                      %mi g
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mrow
                        %mn 4
                        %mo −
                        %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star_g \,\colon\, \Omega^p(X) \longrightarrow \Omega^{4-p}(X)
              denotes the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+star+operator"} Hodge star operator
              induced by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+metric"} pseudo-Riemannian metric
              of the given
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentzian+manifold"} Lorentzian manifold
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,g)
          %p
            Often this is considered for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 3
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \simeq \mathbb{R}^{3,1}
            being
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo =
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g = \eta
            the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+metric"} Minkowski metric
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,g)
            is any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentzian+manifold"} Lorentzian manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Maxwell+theory"} Einstein-Maxwell theory
            which involve the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            and its coupling to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"} background
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+equations"} Einstein equations
            for the metric field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            sourced by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stress-energy+tensor"} stress-energy tensor
            of the Maxwell field.)
          %p
            In order to bring out more manifestly that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+metric"} pseudo-Riemannian metric
            %em only
            through the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+star+operator"} Hodge star operator
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:MaxwellEquationsInTraditionalDifferentialForm"} (1)
            equivalently as follows:
          #eq:MaxwellEquationsInPreMetricDifferentialForm.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtable{:columnalign => "right center left", :displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                    %mtr
                      %mtd
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi F
                      %mtd
                        %mo =
                      %mtd
                        %mn 0
                        %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                          %mrow
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mo ,
                    %mtr
                      %mtd
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi G
                      %mtd
                        %mo =
                      %mtd
                        %mi j
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mtext and
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi G
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mi g
                  %mi F
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{array}{rcl}
                  \\mathrm{d}\,  F &amp;=&amp; 0 \mathrlap{\,,}
                  \\\
                  \\mathrm{d} \, G &amp;=&amp; j
                  \\end{array}
                  \\;\;\;\;
                  \\text{and}
                  \\;\;\;\;
                  G \,=\, \star_g F
                  \\,.
          %h3#electromagnetism_in_dielectric_media Electromagnetism in dielectric media
          %p
            In fact, this formulation
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:MaxwellEquationsInPreMetricDifferentialForm"} (2)
            is closer to the (original) formulation of Maxwell’s equations used in the case that spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is thought of as possibly filled with a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dielectric+medium"} dielectric medium
            where,
            = succeed "," do
              %em a priori
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric+field"} electric field
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec E
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+field"} magnetic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+density"} flux density
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec B
              %p
                which jointly constitute the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Faraday+tensor"} Faraday tensor
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+chart"} coordinate chart
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi t
                        %mo ,
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mn 2
                        %mo ,
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mn 3
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (t,x^1, x^2, x^3)
                = succeed "):" do
                  %a{:href => "Maxwell's+equations#InTermsOfFaradayTensor"} there
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi i
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mi t
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo +
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                      %mi k
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mi i
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi j
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F
                  \\,=\,
                  E_i \mathrm{d}x^i \wedge \mathrm{d} t
                  \\,+\,
                  \\epsilon_{i j k} B^i \mathrm{d} x^j \mathrm{d} x^k
          %p as well as
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the actual
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+field"} magnetic field
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi H
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec H
                and the
                %em dielectric displacement
                (or similar)
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi D
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec D
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo =
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi H
                        %mi i
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                      %mi t
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo −
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mi j
                          %mi k
                      %msup
                        %mi D
                        %mi i
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi j
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi k
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      G
                      \\,=\,
                      H_i \mathrm{d}x^i \wedge \mathrm{d} t
                      \\,-\,
                      \\epsilon_{i j k} D^i \mathrm{d} x^j \mathrm{d} x^k
                      \\,,
          %p
            and then imposes
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constitutive+equations"} constitutive equations
            relating
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\vec E, \vec B)
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\vec H, \vec D)
            and thereby expressing the
            = succeed "-property" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dielectric"} dielectric
            %em medium
            that one imagines filling the spacetime
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            In general,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constitutive+equations"} constitutive equations
            can be nonlinear (and even
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multi-valued+function"} multi-valued
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strengths"} field strengths
            they are of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mi ϵ
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  G \,=\, \star_\epsilon F
          %p
            for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+map"} linear map
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mi ϵ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\star_\epsilon
                  \\,\colon\,
                  \\Omega^2(X)
                  \\longrightarrow
                  \\Omega^2(X)
          %p
            much like the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+star+operator"} Hodge star operator
            (whence here we use similar notation for both, which is non-standard).
          %h2#dualitysymmetric_higher_gauge_theory Duality-symmetric higher gauge theory
          %p
            While not traditionally discussed under this terminology in the literature, we may recognize (
            %a{:href => "#SS23"}> SS23
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+field"} higher
            versions of “premetric” electromagnetism occuring in the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            = succeed "-literature" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %em duality-symmetric
            or “democratic” formulations.
          %center
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/SelfDualHigherFluxEOMs-231221.jpg", :width => "750"}/
          %p
            For more on the general picture see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gauss+law"} Gauss law
                –
                %a{:href => "Gauss+law#InHigherGaugeTheory"} In higher gauge theory
          %h3#RRFieldInGravitationalBackground RR-fields in gravitational backgrounds
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+supergravity"} type II
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D10+supergravity"} D=10 supergravity
            famously contains
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+fields"} higher gauge fields
            called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ramond-Ramond+fields"} Ramond-Ramond fields
            or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-fields"} RR-fields
            for short, which may be understood as a certain higher-degree generalization of the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
          %p
            Where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            is a single
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+2-form"} differential 2-form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ∈
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_2 \,\in\, \Omega^2(X)
            (the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Faraday+tensor"} Faraday tensor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-fields"} RR-fields
            have their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strengths"} field strengths
            encoded in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tuple"} tuple
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms"} differential forms
            in every second degree up to
            %em half
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension+of+a+manifold"} dimension
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi p
                      %mo +
                      %mi σ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ∈
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi p
                      %mo +
                      %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} |
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ≤
                  %mn 2
                  %mi p
                  %mo +
                  %mi κ
                  %mo ≤
                  %mn 5
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} }
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Big\{
                  F_{2p + \sigma}
                  \\,\in\,
                  \\Omega^{2p+\sigma}(X)
                  \\;{\Big\vert}\;
                  0 \leq 2p+\kappa \leq 5
                  \\Big\}
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma = 0
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIA+supergravity"} type IIA supergravity
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma = 1
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIB+supergravity"} type IIB supergravity
          %p
            Moreover, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            of these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-fields"} RR-fields
            are of the general form of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxwell%27s+equations"} Maxwell's equations
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:MaxwellEquationsInTraditionalDifferentialForm"} (1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"} Kalb-Ramond field
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor+fields"} spinor fields
            vanish, then the equations of motion are as before, up to form degree
          #eq:RRFieldEquationsInTraditionalDifferentialForm.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (3)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtable{:columnalign => "right center left", :displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                    %mtr
                      %mtd
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mrow
                            %mn 2
                            %mi p
                            %mo +
                            %mi σ
                      %mtd
                        %mo =
                      %mtd
                        %mn 0
                    %mtr
                      %mtd
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                        %msub
                          %mo ⋆
                          %mi g
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mrow
                            %mn 2
                            %mi p
                            %mo +
                            %mi σ
                      %mtd
                        %mo =
                      %mtd
                        %mn 0
                        %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                          %mrow
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{array}{rcl}
                  \\mathrm{d} F_{2p + \sigma} &amp;=&amp; 0
                  \\\
                  \\mathrm{d} \star_g F_{2p + \sigma} &amp;=&amp; 0
                  \\mathrlap{\,.}
                  \\end{array}
          %p
            (In more generality the right-hand sides are non-vanishing combinations with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"} Kalb-Ramond field
            – already for the first equation! – and the
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor+fields"} spinor fields
          %p
            But one may famously understand
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+supergravity"} type II supergravity
            as the low-energy limit of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quantization+laws"} flux quantization laws
            of the total
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux"} flux
            of these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-fields"} RR-fields
            (in variation of the
            = succeed "-law" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+charge+quantization"} Dirac charge quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetism
            as the background field for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum"} quantum
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electrons"} electrons
          %p
            A widely-considered
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conjecture"} conjecture
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+classification+of+D-brane+charge"}> here
            ) says that this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quantization"} flux quantization
            is realized by
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                regarding the would-be
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+duality"} Hodge duals
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ⋆
                        %mi g
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mi p
                          %mo +
                          %mi σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star_g F_{2p + \sigma}
                of the RR-fields as independent fields
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mrow
                            %mn 10
                            %mo −
                            %mn 2
                            %mi p
                            %mo −
                            %mi σ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{10-2p-\sigma}
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mi p
                          %mo +
                          %mi σ
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ∈
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mi p
                          %mo +
                          %mi σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} |
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ≤
                      %mn 2
                      %mi p
                      %mo +
                      %mi κ
                      %mo ≤
                      %mn 5
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} }
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\Big\{
                      F_{2p + \sigma}
                      \\,\in\,
                      \\Omega^{2p+\sigma}(X)
                      \\;{\Big\vert}\;
                      0 \leq 2p+\kappa \leq 5
                      \\Big\}
                      \\,,
              %p
                and then regarding/constraining such a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tuple"} tuple
                as the image under the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+character"} Chern character
                of a class in (
