          %h2#References References
          %h3#ReferencesPremetricElectromagnetism Premetric electromagnetic fields
          %p The idea (if not the terminology) of pre-metric electromagnetism is attributed to:
          %ul
            %li#Kottler22a
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Friedrich+Kottler"}> Friedrich Kottler
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Newtonsches Gesetz und Metrik
                %strong 131
                (1922) 1-14 &amp;lbrack;Engl. transl. by
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+H.+Delphenich"} Delphenich
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.neo-classical-physics.info/uploads/3/4/3/6/34363841/kottler_-_newtons_law_and_metrics.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Kottler-NewtonsLawAndMetric.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li#Kottler22b
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Friedrich+Kottler"}> Friedrich Kottler
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Maxwell’sche Gleichungen und Metrik
                %strong 131
                (1922) 119-146 &amp;lbrack;Engl. transl. by
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+H.+Delphenich"} Delphenich
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.neo-classical-physics.info/uploads/3/4/3/6/34363841/kottler_-_maxwells_equation.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Kottler-MaxwellEquationsAndMetric.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li#Cartan24
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%89lie+Cartan"}> Élie Cartan
                , §80 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Sur les variétés à connexion affine, et la théorie de la relativité généralisée (première partie) (Suite)
                %strong 41
                (1924) 1-25 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item?id=ASENS_1924_3_41__1_0"}> numdam:ASENS_1924_3_41__1_0
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#Dantzig34
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+van+Dantzig"}> David van Dantzig
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The fundamental equations of electromagnetism, independent of metrical geometry
                %strong 30
                4 (1934) 421-427 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0305004100012664"}> doi:10.1017/S0305004100012664
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p Early review:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edmund+T.+Whittaker"}> Edmund T. Whittaker
                \: pp. 192-196 of:
                %em A History of the Theories of Aether and Electricity
                – Vol. 2:
                %em The Modern Theories 1900-1926
                (1953), reprinted by Humanities Press (1973) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://isidore.co/CalibreLibrary/Whittaker,%20Edmund%20Taylor/A%20History%20of%20the%20Theories%20of%20Aether%20and%20Electricity_%20The%20Modern%20Theories%201900-1926%20(4500)/A%20History%20of%20the%20Theories%20of%20Aether%20and%20El%20-%20Whittaker,%20Edmund%20Taylor.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_History_of_the_Theories_of_Aether_and_Electricity"} Wikipedia entry
              %blockquote
                %p
                  “&amp;lbrack;…&amp;rbrack; This chapter has been concerned, for the most part, with General Relativity, which is essentially a geometrisation of physics. It may be closed with some account of a movement in the opposite direction, seeking to abolish the priviledged position of geometry in physics, and indeed inquiring how far it may be possible to construct a physics independent of geometry. Since the notion of metric is a complicated one, which requires measurements with clocks and scales, generally with
                  %em rigid
                  bodies, which themselves are systems of great complexity, it seems undesirable to take metric as fundamental, particularly for phenomena which are simpler and actually independent of it.”
                %p
                  “The movement was initiated by Friedrich Kottler of Vienna, who in 1922 published two papers &amp;lbrack;
                  %a{:href => "#Kottler22a"}> Kottler (1922a)
                  ,
                  = succeed "&rbrack;\u201D" do
                    %a{:href => "#Kottler22b"} (1922b)
          %p
            Further amplification of the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constitutive+equation"} constitutive
              map
            that relates the pre-metrically independent fields:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E.+Jan+Post"}> E. Jan Post
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The constitutive map and some of its ramifications
              %strong 71
              2 (1972) 497-518 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0003-4916(72)90129-7"}> doi:10.1016/0003-4916(72)90129-7
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            The terminology “pre-metric electromagnetism” seems to be due to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+H.+Delphenich"} David H. Delphenich
          %ul
